/-!
# Support functions of level sets

Let $E$ and $F$ be real vector spaces paired by $B$, and let $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ have
conjugate $f^*$. This module proves three results of §13 of [rockafellar1970convex] that need both
the conjugate and the recession function $f 0^+$. The first is the **level-set duality**: for a
closed convex $f$, the support function of the level set $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$ is
$\operatorname{cl} k$, where $k$ is the positively homogeneous convex function generated by $f^*$
(Theorem 13.5 of [rockafellar1970convex]). The second computes the lineality space of $f^*$: it is
the annihilator of the subspace parallel to the affine hull of $\operatorname{dom} f$ (Theorem 13.4
of [rockafellar1970convex]). The third is co-finiteness: a closed proper convex $f$ has
$f 0^+(y) = +\infty$ for every $y \ne 0$ if and only if $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ is dense, and, in
finite dimensions, if and only if $\operatorname{dom} f^* = F$ (Corollary 13.3.1 of
[rockafellar1970convex]).

The **positively homogeneous convex function generated by** $f$ (§5 of [rockafellar1970convex]) is
the greatest positively homogeneous convex function $k \le f$ with $k(0) \le 0$. It is the function
determined by the convex cone generated by $\operatorname{epi} f$, and for convex $f$ and $x \ne 0$
it is $k(x) = \inf \{\lambda f(\lambda^{-1} x) \mid \lambda > 0\}$. This function lives on the same
space as $f$. The homogenization `ConvexAnalysis.hom` of `Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Homogenize` is this
operator applied to the level-one lift of $f$ to $\mathbb{R} \times E$. The module also uses §14 of
[rockafellar1970convex].

## Main definitions

* `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGenCone`: the cone $\{0\} \cup \bigcup_{a > 0} a \operatorname{epi} f$
  generated by the epigraph. For convex $f$ it is the convex cone `PointedCone.hull ℝ (epi f)`.
* `ConvexAnalysis.Cofinite`: a closed proper convex function whose recession function is $+\infty$
  in every nonzero direction.

## Main results

* `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen_eq_ofEpi`: for convex $f$, the positively homogeneous convex function
  `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen f` generated by $f$ is the function determined by the cone
  `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGenCone f`.
* `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen_apply_of_ne_zero`: for convex $f$ and $x \ne 0$, $k(x) = \inf \{\lambda
  f(\lambda^{-1} x) \mid \lambda > 0\}$. The value at the origin is $0$ if $f(0) \ge 0$ and
  $-\infty$ if $f(0) < 0$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.setOf_convexCl_posHomGen_le_zero`: in finite dimensions, for closed proper convex
  $f$ with $f(0) > 0 > \inf f$, the set $\{x \mid (\operatorname{cl} k)(x) \le 0\}$ is the closed
  convex cone generated by $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.convexConj_posHomGen`: $k^* = \delta(\cdot \mid \{y \mid f^*(y) \le 0\})$, with no
  hypothesis on $f$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.convexConj_apply_zero`, `ConvexAnalysis.iInf_convexConj_eq_neg_apply_zero`:
  $f^*(0) = -\inf f$ for every $f$, and $\inf f^* = -f(0)$ for closed convex $f$. Together they say
  that the hypothesis $f(0) > 0 > \inf f$ is self-dual.
* `ConvexAnalysis.supportFn_setOf_le_zero`, `ConvexAnalysis.convexCl_posHomGen`: the two halves of
  the level-set duality, $\delta^*(\cdot \mid \{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}) = \operatorname{cl} k_{f^*}$
  for closed convex $f$, and $\operatorname{cl} k_g = \delta^*(\cdot \mid \{x \mid g^*(x) \le 0\})$
  for every $g$ (Theorem 13.5 of [rockafellar1970convex]). `ConvexAnalysis.convexCl_hom` is the same
  for the homogenization `ConvexAnalysis.hom f`, for the product pairing on $\mathbb{R} \times E$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.mem_linealitySpaceFn_convexConj_iff`,
  `ConvexAnalysis.linealitySpaceFn_convexConj_eq_annihilator`: $y$ is a direction in which $f^*$ is
  affine if and only if $\langle \cdot, y \rangle$ is constant on $\operatorname{dom} f$, so the
  lineality space of $f^*$ is the annihilator of the subspace parallel to
  $\operatorname{aff} (\operatorname{dom} f)$ (Theorem 13.4 of [rockafellar1970convex]).
* `ConvexAnalysis.interior_convexDom_convexConj_nonempty_iff`: in finite dimensions,
  $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ has interior points if and only if $f$ is affine along no line.
* `ConvexAnalysis.closure_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff`,
  `ConvexAnalysis.cofinite_iff_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ`: $f$ is co-finite if and only if
  $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ is dense, and, in finite dimensions, if and only if
  $\operatorname{dom} f^* = F$ (Corollary 13.3.1 of [rockafellar1970convex]).
* `ConvexAnalysis.mem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff` and its companions for the relative
  interior, the interior and the affine hull: the position of a point $y_0$ relative to
  $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ is decided by the sign of $f 0^+ - \langle \cdot, y_0 \rangle$.

## Notation

* $k$, or $k_f$: the positively homogeneous convex function `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen f` generated
  by $f$.
* $f 0^+$: the recession function `ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn f`.
* $\delta(\cdot \mid C)$ and $\delta^*(\cdot \mid C)$: the indicator function
  `ConvexAnalysis.indicatorFn C` and the support function `ConvexAnalysis.supportFn B C`.

## Implementation notes

* The level-set duality needs no properness. The improper cases are covered by the general
  identification of the closure of a positively homogeneous convex function with a support function,
  which covers $\operatorname{cl} g \equiv -\infty$ as the support function of $\emptyset$.
* Several statements assume `SeparatingDual ℝ E`. The fact "$\delta^*(y \mid F) = +\infty$ for
  $y \ne 0$" needs the pairing to separate the points of $E$: with the indiscrete topology on $E$,
  every support function vanishes. `ConvexAnalysis.separatingRight_flip_of_separatingDual` and
  `ConvexAnalysis.injective_of_separatingDual` are the two forms that later results use.
* One direction of the co-finiteness theorem assumes `FiniteDimensional ℝ F`. The step of
  [rockafellar1970convex] from "$\operatorname{dom} f^*$ is contained in no closed half-space" to
  "$\operatorname{dom} f^*$ is the whole space" fails in infinite dimensions, where the kernel of a
  discontinuous linear functional is a dense proper convex subset. The form with the closure,
  `ConvexAnalysis.closure_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff`, holds without this assumption.
-/

open Set Pointwise

namespace ConvexAnalysis

/-! ### The positively homogeneous convex function generated by $f$ -/

section PosHomGen

variable {E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] {f g : E → EReal} {K : Set (E × ℝ)}
  {a : ℝ} {x : E} {p : E × ℝ}

/-- The **cone generated by the epigraph** of $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$: the set $$\{0\} \cup
\bigcup_{a > 0} a \operatorname{epi} f \subseteq E \times \mathbb{R}.$$

For convex $f$, the function determined by this cone is the positively homogeneous convex
function generated by $f$ (`ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen_eq_ofEpi`). The cone need not be an
epigraph: when $f(0) = +\infty$, it meets the fiber over $0$ in the single point $0$. In the
same way, `ConvexAnalysis.homCone` meets the fiber over the origin of $\mathbb{R} \times E$ in
the single point $0$. -/
def posHomGenCone (f : E → EReal) : Set (E × ℝ) := {0} ∪ ⋃ a > (0 : ℝ), a • epi f

/-- A point $p \in E \times \mathbb{R}$ lies in the cone generated by $\operatorname{epi} f$ if and
only if $p = 0$ or $p \in a \operatorname{epi} f$ for some $a > 0$. -/
theorem mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists :
    p ∈ posHomGenCone f ↔ p = 0 ∨ ∃ a > (0 : ℝ), p ∈ a • epi f := by
  simp [posHomGenCone]

/-- The origin lies in the cone generated by $\operatorname{epi} f$. -/
theorem zero_mem_posHomGenCone (f : E → EReal) : (0 : E × ℝ) ∈ posHomGenCone f :=
  mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists.2 (Or.inl rfl)

/-- The epigraph of $f$ is contained in the cone that it generates. -/
theorem epi_subset_posHomGenCone (f : E → EReal) : epi f ⊆ posHomGenCone f := fun _ hp =>
  mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists.2 (Or.inr ⟨1, one_pos, by rwa [one_smul]⟩)

/-- For $a > 0$, the cone generated by $\operatorname{epi} f$ is invariant under multiplication by
$a$. -/
theorem smul_posHomGenCone (ha : 0 < a) (f : E → EReal) :
    a • posHomGenCone f = posHomGenCone f :=
  smul_zero_union_iUnion_smul ha _

/-- If $f$ is convex, the cone generated by $\operatorname{epi} f$ is convex. -/
theorem convex_posHomGenCone (hf : ConvexFn f) : Convex ℝ (posHomGenCone f) := by
  rw [convex_iff_add_mem_of_isCone (isCone_iff_smul_eq.2 fun c hc => smul_posHomGenCone hc f)]
  intro p hp q hq
  rw [mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists] at hp hq ⊢
  rcases hp with rfl | ⟨a, ha, hp⟩
  · rw [zero_add]
    exact hq
  rcases hq with rfl | ⟨b, hb, hq⟩
  · rw [add_zero]
    exact Or.inr ⟨a, ha, hp⟩
  refine Or.inr ⟨a + b, by positivity, ?_⟩
  rw [hf.convex_epi.add_smul ha.le hb.le]
  exact Set.add_mem_add hp hq

/-- **Minimality of the generated cone.** Let $K \subseteq E \times \mathbb{R}$ contain the origin
and satisfy $a K \subseteq K$ for every $a > 0$. If $\operatorname{epi} f \subseteq K$, then the
cone generated by $\operatorname{epi} f$ is contained in $K$. -/
theorem posHomGenCone_subset (h0 : (0 : E × ℝ) ∈ K) (hK : ∀ a : ℝ, 0 < a → a • K ⊆ K)
    (hf : epi f ⊆ K) : posHomGenCone f ⊆ K := by
  intro p hp
  rcases mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists.1 hp with rfl | ⟨a, ha, hpa⟩
  · exact h0
  · exact hK a ha (Set.smul_set_mono hf hpa)

/-- **For convex $f$, the cone generated by the epigraph is its convex cone hull**:
`ConvexAnalysis.posHomGenCone f` equals `PointedCone.hull ℝ (epi f)`.

So `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGenCone` describes the cone that defines `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen`,
and is not a second definition of it. For nonconvex $f$ the two sets differ: the hull adds sums
that the union misses. -/
theorem posHomGenCone_eq_coe_hull (hf : ConvexFn f) :
    posHomGenCone f = (PointedCone.hull ℝ (epi f) : Set (E × ℝ)) := by
  rw [coe_hull_of_convex hf.convex_epi]
  ext q
  rw [mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists, Set.mem_insert_iff]
  constructor
  · rintro (rfl | ⟨a, ha, h⟩)
    · exact Or.inl rfl
    · exact Or.inr ⟨a, ha, h⟩
  · rintro (rfl | ⟨a, ha, h⟩)
    · exact Or.inl rfl
    · exact Or.inr ⟨a, ha, h⟩

/-- The cone `ConvexAnalysis.homCone f` is the cone generated by the epigraph of the level-one lift
`ConvexAnalysis.levelOneLift f`. -/
theorem homCone_eq_posHomGenCone (f : E → EReal) :
    homCone f = posHomGenCone (levelOneLift f) := rfl

/-- **For convex $f$, the cone generated by the epigraph of the level-one lift is its convex cone
hull**, so `ConvexAnalysis.homCone f` is the convex cone generated by the vectors $(1, x, \mu)$
with $\mu \ge f(x)$. -/
theorem homCone_eq_coe_hull (hf : ConvexFn f) :
    homCone f = (PointedCone.hull ℝ (epi (levelOneLift f)) : Set ((ℝ × E) × ℝ)) :=
  posHomGenCone_eq_coe_hull (convexFn_levelOneLift hf)

/-- **The positively homogeneous convex function generated by a convex function**, as the function
determined by a cone. For convex $f$, `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen f` is the function whose value
at $x$ is $\inf \{\mu \mid (x, \mu) \in K\}$, where $K = \{0\} \cup \bigcup_{a > 0} a
\operatorname{epi} f$.

`ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen` itself is defined from the convex cone hull
`PointedCone.hull ℝ (epi f)`, which gives it convexity and maximality with no hypothesis. This
theorem gives the description by rays that the classical proofs use. -/
theorem posHomGen_eq_ofEpi (hf : ConvexFn f) : posHomGen f = ofEpi (posHomGenCone f) := by
  rw [posHomGenCone_eq_coe_hull hf, posHomGen]

/-- For every $f$, the generated function satisfies $k(0) \ne +\infty$. -/
theorem posHomGen_apply_zero_ne_top (f : E → EReal) : posHomGen f 0 ≠ ⊤ :=
  ((posHomGen_apply_zero_le f).trans_lt (by simp)).ne

/-- If $f$ is convex and $f(0) \ge 0$, then the generated function satisfies $k(0) = 0$. -/
theorem posHomGen_apply_zero_of_nonneg (hf : ConvexFn f) (h : 0 ≤ f 0) : posHomGen f 0 = 0 := by
  refine le_antisymm (posHomGen_apply_zero_le f) ?_
  rw [posHomGen_eq_ofEpi hf]
  refine le_ofEpi fun μ hμ => ?_
  rcases mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists.1 hμ with h0 | ⟨a, ha, hmem⟩
  · rw [show μ = 0 from congrArg (fun q : E × ℝ => q.2) h0]
    exact le_of_eq EReal.coe_zero.symm
  · rw [Set.mem_smul_set_iff_inv_smul_mem₀ ha.ne'] at hmem
    have hx : (a⁻¹ • ((0 : E), μ)).1 = (0 : E) := by simp
    have hfle : f 0 ≤ ((a⁻¹ * μ : ℝ) : EReal) := by
      have := mem_epi.1 hmem
      rwa [hx] at this
    have hnn : (0 : ℝ) ≤ a⁻¹ * μ := by
      exact_mod_cast h.trans hfle
    have : (0 : ℝ) ≤ μ := by
      have ha' : 0 < a⁻¹ := inv_pos.2 ha
      nlinarith
    exact_mod_cast this

/-- If $f$ is convex, then the generated function satisfies $k(0) = -\infty$ if and only if
$f(0) < 0$. -/
theorem posHomGen_apply_zero_eq_bot_iff (hf : ConvexFn f) : posHomGen f 0 = ⊥ ↔ f 0 < 0 := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    by_contra hcon
    rw [not_lt] at hcon
    rw [posHomGen_apply_zero_of_nonneg hf hcon] at h
    exact absurd h (by simp)
  · intro h
    have h0 : f 0 < ((0 : ℝ) : EReal) := by rwa [EReal.coe_zero]
    obtain ⟨q, hq₁, hq₂⟩ := EReal.exists_between_coe_real h0
    replace hq₂ := EReal.coe_lt_coe_iff.1 hq₂
    rw [posHomGen_eq_ofEpi hf]
    refine le_bot_iff.1 (EReal.le_of_forall_lt_iff_le.1 fun r _ => ?_)
    set a : ℝ := max (r / q) 1 with ha
    have ha0 : 0 < a := lt_of_lt_of_le one_pos (le_max_right _ _)
    have hmem : ((0 : E), a * q) ∈ posHomGenCone f := by
      refine mem_posHomGenCone_iff_exists.2 (Or.inr ⟨a, ha0, ⟨((0 : E), q), ?_, ?_⟩⟩)
      · exact mk_mem_epi.2 hq₁.le
      · simp [Prod.smul_mk]
    refine (ofEpi_apply_le hmem).trans ?_
    have hle : a * q ≤ r :=
      calc a * q ≤ (r / q) * q := mul_le_mul_of_nonpos_right (le_max_left _ _) hq₂.le
        _ = r := div_mul_cancel₀ r hq₂.ne
    exact_mod_cast hle

/-- Let $K \subseteq E \times \mathbb{R}$ satisfy $a K = K$ for every $a > 0$. Then the function
$x \mapsto \inf \{\mu \mid (x, \mu) \in K\}$ determined by $K$ is positively homogeneous. -/
theorem posHomogeneous_ofEpi (hK : ∀ a : ℝ, 0 < a → a • K = K) : PosHomogeneous (ofEpi K) := by
  have key : ∀ b : ℝ, 0 < b → ∀ y : E, ofEpi K (b • y) ≤ (b : EReal) * ofEpi K y := by
    intro b hb y
    have hmul : (b : EReal) * ofEpi K y
        = ⨅ μ ∈ {μ : ℝ | (y, μ) ∈ K}, ((b * μ : ℝ) : EReal) := by
      simp only [ofEpi, EReal.coe_mul_iInf hb, ← EReal.coe_mul]
    rw [hmul]
    refine le_iInf₂ fun μ hμ => ?_
    have hmem0 : ((y, μ) : E × ℝ) ∈ K := hμ
    have hmem : ((b • y, b * μ) : E × ℝ) ∈ K := by
      rw [← hK b hb]
      have hs := Set.smul_mem_smul_set (a := b) hmem0
      rwa [Prod.smul_mk, smul_eq_mul] at hs
    exact ofEpi_apply_le hmem
  intro a ha x
  refine le_antisymm (key a ha x) ?_
  have h := key a⁻¹ (inv_pos.2 ha) (a • x)
  rw [inv_smul_smul₀ ha.ne'] at h
  have h2 : (a : EReal) * ofEpi K x ≤ (a : EReal) * (((a⁻¹ : ℝ) : EReal) * ofEpi K (a • x)) :=
    mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left h (by exact_mod_cast ha.le)
  rwa [← mul_assoc, ← EReal.coe_mul, mul_inv_cancel₀ ha.ne', EReal.coe_one,
    one_mul] at h2

/-- **The description by rays is enough for maximality.** Let $g : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ be
positively homogeneous with $g(0) \le 0$ and $g \le f$. Then $g$ lies below the function
determined by the cone generated by $\operatorname{epi} f$.

Convexity of $g$ is not needed. The theorem `ConvexAnalysis.le_posHomGen` needs it, because it
works with the convex cone hull. -/
theorem le_ofEpi_posHomGenCone (hg : PosHomogeneous g) (h0 : g 0 ≤ 0) (hle : g ≤ f) :
    g ≤ ofEpi (posHomGenCone f) := by
  refine subset_epi_iff_le_ofEpi.1 (posHomGenCone_subset ?_ ?_ (epi_anti hle))
  · exact mk_mem_epi.2 (by simpa using h0)
  · exact fun a ha => (posHomogeneous_iff_isCone_epi.1 hg a ha).subset

/-- **The formula for the generated function**, away from the origin. Let $f$ be convex and
$x \ne 0$. Then $$k(x) = \inf_{\lambda > 0} \lambda f(\lambda^{-1} x).$$

The origin must be excluded, because the generated cone contributes the point $(0, 0)$ there. -/
theorem posHomGen_apply_of_ne_zero (hf : ConvexFn f) (hx : x ≠ 0) :
    posHomGen f x = ⨅ a > (0 : ℝ), (a : EReal) * f (a⁻¹ • x) := by
  have hsing : ∀ μ : ℝ, (x, μ) ∉ ({0} : Set (E × ℝ)) := fun μ h =>
    hx (congrArg (fun q : E × ℝ => q.1) h)
  have hcongr : ∀ μ : ℝ, (x, μ) ∈ posHomGenCone f ↔ (x, μ) ∈ ⋃ a > (0 : ℝ), a • epi f := by
    intro μ
    rw [posHomGenCone, Set.mem_union]
    exact or_iff_right (hsing μ)
  rw [posHomGen_eq_ofEpi hf, ofEpi_apply_congr hcongr, ofEpi_iUnion, iInf_apply]
  refine iInf_congr fun b => ?_
  rw [ofEpi_iUnion, iInf_apply]
  refine iInf_congr fun hb => ?_
  rw [← smulRight, smulRight_apply_pos hb]

/-- If $f$ is convex, the effective domain of the generated function is $$\operatorname{dom} k =
\{0\} \cup \bigcup_{a > 0} a \operatorname{dom} f.$$
-/
theorem convexDom_posHomGen (hf : ConvexFn f) :
    convexDom (posHomGen f) = {0} ∪ ⋃ a > (0 : ℝ), a • convexDom f := by
  rw [posHomGen_eq_ofEpi hf, convexDom_ofEpi, posHomGenCone, Set.image_union, Set.image_iUnion₂]
  congr 1
  · exact Set.image_singleton
  · refine Set.iUnion₂_congr fun a _ => ?_
    rw [convexDom_eq_fst_image_epi]
    ext z
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨-, ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩, rfl⟩
      exact ⟨q.1, ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩, rfl⟩
    · rintro ⟨-, ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩, rfl⟩
      exact ⟨a • q, ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩, rfl⟩

/-- **The homogenization is the generated function of the level-one lift.** If $f$ is convex and
$\operatorname{dom} f$ is nonempty, then `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen (ConvexAnalysis.levelOneLift
f)` equals `ConvexAnalysis.hom f`, because the two generated cones are the same set.

The hypothesis $f \not\equiv +\infty$ is needed: for $f \equiv +\infty$ the cone degenerates to
$\{0\}$, which determines $\delta(\cdot \mid 0)$, whereas `ConvexAnalysis.hom f` is identically
$+\infty$. -/
theorem posHomGen_levelOneLift (hf : ConvexFn f) (hdom : (convexDom f).Nonempty) :
    posHomGen (levelOneLift f) = hom f := by
  rw [posHomGen_eq_ofEpi (convexFn_levelOneLift hf), ← homCone_eq_posHomGenCone,
    ← hom_eq_ofEpi_homCone hdom]

end PosHomGen

/-!
### The zero level set of the generated function

Two sublevel sets of $k$ enclose the convex cone generated by $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$: the set
$\{k < 0\}$ lies inside it and the set $\{k \le 0\}$ contains it. The reason is that
$(f \lambda)(x) \le 0$ for a positive $\lambda$ says exactly that $\lambda^{-1} x$ lies in
$\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$. Both sublevel sets have the same closure, so the closed convex cone
generated by $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$ is the zero sublevel set of $\operatorname{cl} k$.
-/

section PosHomGenLevel

section Algebraic

variable {E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] {f : E → EReal}

/-- **The zero sublevel set of $k$ is a convex cone.** If $k(x) \ne -\infty$ for every $x$, then the
convex cone generated by $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$ is contained in $\{x \mid k(x) \le 0\}$.

The hypothesis that $k$ never takes the value $-\infty$ is the cost of reading positive
homogeneity together with convexity as subadditivity. -/
theorem coe_hull_setOf_le_zero_subset (hb : ∀ x, posHomGen f x ≠ ⊥) :
    (PointedCone.hull ℝ {x : E | f x ≤ 0} : Set E) ⊆ {x : E | posHomGen f x ≤ 0} := by
  have hsub := ((posHomogeneous_posHomGen f).convexFn_iff_subadditive hb).1 (convexFn_posHomGen f)
  intro x hx
  induction hx using Submodule.span_induction with
  | mem y hy => exact (posHomGen_le f y).trans hy
  | zero => exact posHomGen_apply_zero_le f
  | add y z _ _ hy hz =>
      have hy' : posHomGen f y ≤ 0 := hy
      have hz' : posHomGen f z ≤ 0 := hz
      exact (hsub y z).trans (by simpa using add_le_add hy' hz')
  | smul c y _ hy =>
      rcases eq_or_lt_of_le c.2 with hc | hc
      · have hzero : (c : ℝ) • y = 0 := by rw [← hc, zero_smul]
        change posHomGen f ((c : ℝ) • y) ≤ 0
        rw [hzero]
        exact posHomGen_apply_zero_le f
      · change posHomGen f ((c : ℝ) • y) ≤ 0
        rw [posHomogeneous_posHomGen f (c : ℝ) hc y]
        calc ((c : ℝ) : EReal) * posHomGen f y
            ≤ ((c : ℝ) : EReal) * 0 := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left hy (by exact_mod_cast c.2)
          _ = 0 := mul_zero _

/-- **Where $k$ is negative, the point already lies on a ray through $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$.** If
$f$ is convex and $f(0) \ge 0$, then $\{x \mid k(x) < 0\}$ is contained in the convex cone
generated by $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$.

The reason is that $(f \lambda)(x) \le 0$ for a positive $\lambda$ exactly when
$\lambda^{-1} x \in \{y \mid f(y) \le 0\}$. The hypothesis $f(0) \ge 0$ excludes the origin,
because it forces $k(0) = 0$. -/
theorem setOf_posHomGen_lt_zero_subset (hf : ConvexFn f) (h0 : 0 ≤ f 0) :
    {x : E | posHomGen f x < 0} ⊆ (PointedCone.hull ℝ {x : E | f x ≤ 0} : Set E) := by
  intro x hx
  have hxne : x ≠ 0 := by
    rintro rfl
    rw [Set.mem_ofPred, posHomGen_apply_zero_of_nonneg hf h0] at hx
    exact absurd hx (lt_irrefl 0)
  rw [Set.mem_ofPred, posHomGen_apply_of_ne_zero hf hxne] at hx
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := iInf_lt_iff.1 hx
  obtain ⟨hapos, ha'⟩ := iInf_lt_iff.1 ha
  have hz : f (a⁻¹ • x) ≤ 0 := by
    by_contra hcon
    rw [not_le] at hcon
    have hnn : (0 : EReal) ≤ (a : EReal) * f (a⁻¹ • x) := by
      have h := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left hcon.le (a := ((a : ℝ) : EReal))
        (by exact_mod_cast hapos.le)
      rwa [mul_zero] at h
    exact absurd ha' (not_lt.2 hnn)
  have hmem : a • (a⁻¹ • x) ∈ PointedCone.hull ℝ {y : E | f y ≤ 0} :=
    Submodule.smul_mem _ (⟨a, hapos.le⟩ : {c : ℝ // 0 ≤ c}) (PointedCone.subset_hull hz)
  rwa [smul_inv_smul₀ hapos.ne'] at hmem

end Algebraic

section FiniteDim

variable {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
  {f : E → EReal}

/-- **The closed convex cone generated by a level set is the zero sublevel set of
$\operatorname{cl} k$.** Let $E$ be a finite-dimensional real normed space and
$f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ a closed proper convex function with $f(0) > 0$ and $\inf f < 0$.
Then $$\{x \mid (\operatorname{cl} k)(x) \le 0\} = \operatorname{cl} \operatorname{cone} \{x
\mid f(x) \le 0\},$$ where $\operatorname{cone} S$ is the convex cone generated by $S$ (with the
origin), `PointedCone.hull ℝ S`.

The sets $\{k < 0\}$ and $\{k \le 0\}$ enclose the cone generated by $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$,
and both have closure $\{x \mid (\operatorname{cl} k)(x) \le 0\}$. That step needs $k$ proper
convex with $\inf k < 0$, which is where $f(0) > 0$ and $\inf f < 0$ are used. It is the only
use of finite dimension here. -/
theorem setOf_convexCl_posHomGen_le_zero (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) (h0 : 0 < f 0)
    (hinf : ⨅ x, f x < 0) :
    {x : E | convexCl (posHomGen f) x ≤ 0}
      = closure (PointedCone.hull ℝ {x : E | f x ≤ 0} : Set E) := by
  have hkp : ProperConvex (posHomGen f) :=
      properConvex_posHomGen hf.convex hf.proper hf.isClosed_epi h0
  have hkc : ConvexFn (posHomGen f) := convexFn_posHomGen f
  obtain ⟨x₀, hx₀⟩ := iInf_lt_iff.1 hinf
  have hkinf : ⨅ x, posHomGen f x < ((0 : ℝ) : EReal) := by
    rw [EReal.coe_zero]
    exact lt_of_le_of_lt ((iInf_le _ x₀).trans (posHomGen_le f x₀)) hx₀
  have hle := hkc.closure_setOf_le_convexCl hkp hkinf
  have hlt := hkc.closure_setOf_lt_eq hkinf
  simp only [EReal.coe_zero] at hle hlt
  refine subset_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · rw [← hle, ← hlt]
    exact closure_mono (setOf_posHomGen_lt_zero_subset hf.convex h0.le)
  · rw [← hle]
    exact closure_mono (coe_hull_setOf_le_zero_subset hkp.ne_bot)

end FiniteDim

end PosHomGenLevel

/-!
### The conjugate of a generated positively homogeneous function

This is the algebraic core of the level-set duality, and it needs no topology. The function $k$ is
positively homogeneous, so its conjugate is an indicator function
(`ConvexAnalysis.convexConj_eq_indicatorFn_of_posHomogeneous`), and the set that it indicates does
not change in the passage from $f$ to $k$.
-/

section Conj

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]

/-- For every $y \in F$, the linear function $x \mapsto B(x, y)$ is positively homogeneous as a
function to the extended reals. -/
theorem posHomogeneous_pairing (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (y : F) :
    PosHomogeneous fun x => ((B x y : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  intro a ha x
  simp only [map_smul, LinearMap.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul]
  exact EReal.coe_mul _ _

/-- For every $y \in F$, the linear function $x \mapsto B(x, y)$ is convex as a function to the
extended reals. -/
theorem convexFn_pairing (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (y : F) :
    ConvexFn fun x => ((B x y : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  have h : (fun x => ((B x y : ℝ) : EReal)) = affineFn B y 0 := by
    funext x
    rw [affineFn_apply, EReal.coe_zero, sub_zero]
  rw [h]
  exact convexFn_affineFn y 0

/-- **Homogenization does not change the support set.** For every $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ and
$y \in F$, $B(\cdot, y) \le k$ if and only if $B(\cdot, y) \le f$. There is no hypothesis on
$f$.

The support set is `ConvexAnalysis.supportSet`. -/
theorem supportSet_posHomGen (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (f : E → EReal) :
    supportSet B (posHomGen f) = supportSet B f := by
  ext y
  refine ⟨fun hy x => (hy x).trans (posHomGen_le f x), fun hy => ?_⟩
  exact le_posHomGen (posHomogeneous_pairing B y) (convexFn_pairing B y) (by simp) hy

/-- **The conjugate of the positively homogeneous convex function generated by $f$.** For every
$f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$, $$k^* = \delta(\cdot \mid \{y \mid f^*(y) \le 0\}).$$

There is no hypothesis at all (no closedness, properness or convexity of $f$), because $k$ is
positively homogeneous and nonpositive at the origin for every $f$. -/
theorem convexConj_posHomGen (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (f : E → EReal) :
    convexConj B (posHomGen f) = indicatorFn {y : F | convexConj B f y ≤ 0} := by
  rw [convexConj_eq_indicatorFn_of_posHomogeneous (posHomogeneous_posHomGen f)
      ⟨0, posHomGen_apply_zero_ne_top f⟩, supportSet_posHomGen, supportSet_eq_setOf_convexConj_le]

/-- For every $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$, the zero sublevel sets of $k^*$ and $f^*$ are equal:
$\{y \mid k^*(y) \le 0\} = \{y \mid f^*(y) \le 0\}$. -/
theorem setOf_convexConj_posHomGen_le_zero (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (f : E → EReal) :
    {y : F | convexConj B (posHomGen f) y ≤ 0} = {y : F | convexConj B f y ≤ 0} := by
  rw [← supportSet_eq_setOf_convexConj_le, ← supportSet_eq_setOf_convexConj_le,
      supportSet_posHomGen]

/-- **The conjugate at the origin is minus the infimum.** For every $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$,
$$f^*(0) = -\inf_x f(x).$$ There is no hypothesis on $f$.
-/
theorem convexConj_apply_zero (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (f : E → EReal) :
    convexConj B f 0 = -⨅ x, f x := by
  rw [convexConj_apply, EReal.neg_iInf]
  refine iSup_congr fun x => ?_
  rw [map_zero (B x), EReal.coe_zero]
  exact zero_add _

end Conj

section ConjZero

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] {f : E → EReal}

/-- **The infimum of the conjugate is minus the value at the origin.** Let $E$ be a locally convex
space and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with the topology of $E$. If
$f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ is convex and closed, then $$\inf_y f^*(y) = -f(0).$$

With `ConvexAnalysis.convexConj_apply_zero`, this says that the hypothesis $f(0) > 0 > \inf f$
is self-dual. -/
theorem iInf_convexConj_eq_neg_apply_zero (hf : ConvexFn f) (hc : ClosedConvex f) :
    ⨅ y, convexConj B f y = -f 0 := by
  have h : convexConj B.flip (convexConj B f) 0 = -⨅ y, convexConj B f y :=
      convexConj_apply_zero B.flip (convexConj B f)
  rw [show convexConj B.flip (convexConj B f) = convexBiconj B f from rfl,
      convexBiconj_eq_self hf hc] at h
  rw [h, neg_neg]

end ConjZero

/-!
### The support function of a zero level set

Only the space on which the function lives carries a topology: the closure of a positively
homogeneous convex function is a support function, and the level set is a subset of the other space.
-/

section ZeroLevelSupport

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] {g : F → EReal}

/-- **The closure of the generated function is a support function.** Let $F$ be a locally convex
space and let the flipped pairing be compatible with the topology of $F$. For every
$g : F \to [-\infty, +\infty]$, $$\operatorname{cl} k_g = \delta^*(\cdot \mid \{x \in E \mid
g^*(x) \le 0\}),$$ where $g^*$ is the conjugate for the flipped pairing.

There is no hypothesis at all, not even convexity of $g$. The improper case is covered: there
both sides are the support function of $\emptyset$. -/
theorem convexCl_posHomGen (g : F → EReal) :
    convexCl (posHomGen g) = supportFn B {x : E | convexConj B.flip g x ≤ 0} := by
  rw [convexCl_eq_supportFn_of_posHomogeneous (B := B) (posHomogeneous_posHomGen g)
      (convexFn_posHomGen g) ⟨0, posHomGen_apply_zero_ne_top g⟩, supportSet_posHomGen,
    supportSet_eq_setOf_convexConj_le]

/-- **The same with the closure removed.** Under the hypotheses of
`ConvexAnalysis.convexCl_posHomGen`, if $k_g$ is closed, then $k_g = \delta^*(\cdot \mid \{x \in
E \mid g^*(x) \le 0\})$. -/
theorem supportFn_setOf_convexConj_le_zero (hcl : ClosedConvex (posHomGen g)) :
    supportFn B {x : E | convexConj B.flip g x ≤ 0} = posHomGen g :=
  (convexCl_posHomGen g).symm.trans hcl

end ZeroLevelSupport

section

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] {f : E → EReal}

/-- **The support function of a level set.** Let $E$ and $F$ be locally convex spaces, and let the
pairing $B$ be compatible with the topologies of both $E$ and $F$. If
$f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ is convex and closed, then $$\delta^*(\cdot \mid \{x \mid f(x)
\le 0\}) = \operatorname{cl} k_{f^*}.$$ This is Theorem 13.5 of [rockafellar1970convex].

Closedness of $f$ turns $\{x \mid f^{**}(x) \le 0\}$ back into $\{x \mid f(x) \le 0\}$. The
properness hypothesis of the book is not needed, because `ConvexAnalysis.convexBiconj_eq_self`
covers the improper cases too. -/
theorem supportFn_setOf_le_zero (hf : ConvexFn f) (hc : ClosedConvex f) :
    supportFn B {x : E | f x ≤ 0} = convexCl (posHomGen (convexConj B f)) := by
  have hset : {x : E | convexConj B.flip (convexConj B f) x ≤ 0} = {x : E | f x ≤ 0} := by
    rw [show convexConj B.flip (convexConj B f) = convexBiconj B f from rfl,
        convexBiconj_eq_self hf hc]
  rw [convexCl_posHomGen (B := B) (convexConj B f), hset]

end

/-!
### The homogenization

The previous result, applied to the level-one lift of $f$, for the pairing of $\mathbb{R} \times E$
with $\mathbb{R} \times F$ given by $\langle (\lambda, x), (\lambda^*, x^*) \rangle = \lambda
\lambda^* + \langle x, x^* \rangle$. The factor $\mathbb{R}$ is paired with itself by
multiplication, which is `innerₗ ℝ`. Up to a positive scalar, this is the only compatible pairing of
$\mathbb{R}$ with $\mathbb{R}$, and it is the one that $\lambda \lambda^*$ in the book means.
-/

section Homogenisation

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] {f : E → EReal}

omit [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E]
  [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E] [IsCompatiblePairing B] in
/-- Let $h$ be the level-one lift of $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$, with $h(1, x) = f(x)$ and
$h = +\infty$ elsewhere. For every $(\lambda^*, x^*) \in \mathbb{R} \times F$, $$h^*(\lambda^*,
x^*) \le 0 \iff f^*(x^*) \le -\lambda^*,$$ where $h^*$ is the conjugate for the pairing
$\lambda \lambda^* + \langle x, x^* \rangle$.

This is the computation $h^*(\lambda^*, x^*) = \lambda^* + f^*(x^*)$, stated as a condition on a
sublevel set instead of an equation, to avoid the sum $+\infty + (-\infty)$ of extended reals. -/
theorem convexConj_levelOneLift_le_zero_iff (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (f : E → EReal) (q : ℝ × F) :
    convexConj (prodPairing (innerₗ ℝ) B) (levelOneLift f) q ≤ 0 ↔
      convexConj B f q.2 ≤ ((-q.1 : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  have hcoef : ∀ p : ℝ × E, p.1 = 1 →
      affineFn (prodPairing (innerₗ ℝ) B) q 0 p = affineFn B q.2 (-q.1) p.2 := by
    intro p hp
    rw [affineFn_apply, affineFn_apply, ← EReal.coe_sub, ← EReal.coe_sub]
    congr 1
    simp only [prodPairing_apply, sub_zero, sub_neg_eq_add, hp]
    have h1 : innerₗ ℝ (1 : ℝ) q.1 = q.1 := by simp
    rw [h1, add_comm]
  rw [show ((0 : EReal)) = ((0 : ℝ) : EReal) from EReal.coe_zero.symm, convexConj_le_coe_iff,
    convexConj_le_coe_iff, Pi.le_def, Pi.le_def]
  constructor
  · intro h x
    have hx := h ((1 : ℝ), x)
    rwa [hcoef ((1 : ℝ), x) rfl, levelOneLift_apply_one] at hx
  · intro h p
    by_cases hp : p.1 = 1
    · rw [hcoef p hp, levelOneLift_of_fst_eq_one hp]
      exact h p.2
    · rw [levelOneLift_of_fst_ne_one hp]
      exact le_top

/-- **The closure of the homogenization is a support function.** Let $E$ be a locally convex space
and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with the topology of $E$. If
$f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ is convex and $\operatorname{dom} f$ is nonempty, then
$$\operatorname{cl} (\operatorname{hom} f) = \delta^*\bigl(\cdot \mid \{(\lambda^*, x^*) \in
\mathbb{R} \times F \mid \lambda^* \le -f^*(x^*)\}\bigr),$$ for the pairing
$\lambda \lambda^* + \langle x, x^* \rangle$ of $\mathbb{R} \times E$ with
$\mathbb{R} \times F$.

The condition $\lambda^* \le -f^*(x^*)$ is stated as $f^*(x^*) \le -\lambda^*$. The book states
the conclusion for the explicit function $k(\lambda, x) = (f \lambda)(x)$ for $\lambda > 0$,
$(f 0^+)(x)$ for $\lambda = 0$ and $+\infty$ for $\lambda < 0$; the identification of that $k$
with $\operatorname{cl} (\operatorname{hom} f)$ is a separate statement about recession
functions. -/
theorem convexCl_hom (hf : ConvexFn f) (hdom : (convexDom f).Nonempty) :
    convexCl (hom f) = supportFn (prodPairing (innerₗ ℝ) B).flip
      {q : ℝ × F | convexConj B f q.2 ≤ ((-q.1 : ℝ) : EReal)} := by
  rw [← posHomGen_levelOneLift hf hdom,
    convexCl_posHomGen (B := (prodPairing (innerₗ ℝ) B).flip) (levelOneLift f)]
  refine congrArg (supportFn (prodPairing (innerₗ ℝ) B).flip) (Set.ext fun q => ?_)
  simp only [Set.mem_ofPred_eq, LinearMap.flip_flip]
  exact convexConj_levelOneLift_le_zero_iff B f q

end Homogenisation

/-!
### The lineality space of the conjugate

The **lineality space of a function** (`ConvexAnalysis.linealitySpaceFn`) is the set of directions
$y$ in which the recession function is additively reversible, $(f 0^+)(-y) = -(f 0^+)(y)$: the
directions of affineness of [rockafellar1970convex]. It is strictly larger than the **constancy
space** (`ConvexAnalysis.constancySpace`), which asks the recession function to vanish in both
directions. This section computes the lineality space.
-/

section ConjLineality

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} {f : E → EReal}

/-- **$y$ is a direction of affineness of $f^*$ exactly when $\langle \cdot, y \rangle$ is constant
on $\operatorname{dom} f$.** Let $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ be proper, with $f^*$ proper.
For $y \in F$, $y$ lies in the lineality space of $f^*$ if and only if there is
$c \in \mathbb{R}$ with $$B(x, y) = c \quad \text{for every } x \in \operatorname{dom} f.$$
-/
theorem mem_linealitySpaceFn_convexConj_iff (hp : ProperConvex f)
    (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) {y : F} :
    y ∈ linealitySpaceFn (convexConj B f) ↔ ∃ c : ℝ, ∀ x ∈ convexDom f, B x y = c := by
  rw [mem_linealitySpaceFn, recessionFn_convexConj hp hc]
  exact supportFn_neg_eq_neg_iff hp.convexDom_nonempty y

/-- Let $f$ and $f^*$ be proper. The lineality space of $f^*$ is the set of $y \in F$ with
$B(x_1, y) = B(x_2, y)$ for all $x_1, x_2 \in \operatorname{dom} f$. -/
theorem linealitySpaceFn_convexConj (hp : ProperConvex f) (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) :
    linealitySpaceFn (convexConj B f) =
        {y : F | ∀ x₁ ∈ convexDom f, ∀ x₂ ∈ convexDom f, B x₁ y = B x₂ y} := by
  ext y
  rw [mem_linealitySpaceFn_convexConj_iff hp hc]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨c, hc'⟩ x₁ h₁ x₂ h₂ => by rw [hc' x₁ h₁, hc' x₂ h₂], fun h => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨x₀, hx₀⟩ := hp.convexDom_nonempty
  exact ⟨B x₀ y, fun x hx => h x hx x₀ hx₀⟩

/-- **The lineality space of $f^*$ is the annihilator of the subspace parallel to
$\operatorname{aff} (\operatorname{dom} f)$.** Let $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ be proper,
with $f^*$ proper. Then the lineality space of $f^*$ is $$\{y \in F \mid B(v, y) = 0 \text{ for
every } v \in \operatorname{span} (\operatorname{dom} f - \operatorname{dom} f)\}.$$ This is
Theorem 13.4 of [rockafellar1970convex].

The subspace parallel to $\operatorname{aff} (\operatorname{dom} f)$ is
`vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom f)`. In an inner product space the annihilator is the orthogonal
complement, which is the phrasing of the book. -/
theorem linealitySpaceFn_convexConj_eq_annihilator (hp : ProperConvex f)
    (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) :
    linealitySpaceFn (convexConj B f) = {y : F | ∀ v ∈ vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom f), B v y = 0} := by
  rw [linealitySpaceFn_convexConj hp hc]
  ext y
  constructor
  · intro h v hv
    have hle : vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom f) ≤ LinearMap.ker (B.flip y) := by
      rw [vectorSpan_def, Submodule.span_le]
      intro w hw
      obtain ⟨x₁, hx₁, x₂, hx₂, rfl⟩ := Set.mem_vsub.1 hw
      have hzero : B (x₁ - x₂) y = 0 := by
        rw [map_sub, LinearMap.sub_apply, h x₁ hx₁ x₂ hx₂, sub_self]
      simpa [vsub_eq_sub] using hzero
    exact hle hv
  · intro h x₁ h₁ x₂ h₂
    have hv : x₁ - x₂ ∈ vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom f) := by
      have hm := vsub_mem_vectorSpan ℝ h₁ h₂
      rwa [vsub_eq_sub] at hm
    have hz := h _ hv
    rw [map_sub, LinearMap.sub_apply] at hz
    linarith

end ConjLineality

section LinealityDual

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] {f : E → EReal}

/-- The dual form. Let $E$ be a locally convex space and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with the
topology of $E$. For a closed proper convex $f$, the lineality space of $f$ is the annihilator
of the subspace parallel to $\operatorname{aff} (\operatorname{dom} f^*)$: $$\{x \in E \mid B(x,
w) = 0 \text{ for every } w \in \operatorname{span} (\operatorname{dom} f^* - \operatorname{dom}
f^*)\}.$$
-/
theorem linealitySpaceFn_eq_annihilator_convexDom_convexConj (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    linealitySpaceFn f = {x : E | ∀ w ∈ vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)), B x w = 0} := by
  have hcp : ProperConvex (convexConj B f) := properConvex_convexConj hf
  have hbi : convexConj B.flip (convexConj B f) = f := convexBiconj_eq_self hf.convex hf.closed
  have h := linealitySpaceFn_convexConj_eq_annihilator (B := B.flip) hcp
    (by rw [hbi]; exact hf.proper)
  rw [hbi] at h
  rw [h]
  ext x
  exact forall₂_congr fun w _ => Iff.rfl

end LinealityDual

/-! ### Co-finiteness -/

section Cofinite

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} {f : E → EReal}

/-- A **co-finite** function is a closed proper convex function $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ whose
epigraph contains no nonvertical half-line, that is, whose recession function satisfies
$(f 0^+)(y) = +\infty$ for every $y \ne 0$.

In finite dimensions these are exactly the functions with a conjugate that is finite everywhere
(`ConvexAnalysis.cofinite_iff_forall_convexConj_lt_top`). -/
structure Cofinite (f : E → EReal) : Prop extends ClosedProperConvexFn f where
  /-- The recession function is $+\infty$ in every direction except $0$. -/
  recessionFn_eq_top : ∀ y : E, y ≠ 0 → recessionFn f y = ⊤

end Cofinite

section CofiniteMain

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] {f : E → EReal}

omit [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] in
/-- Let $E$ carry a topology for which it has separating dual, and let the pairing $B$ be compatible
with this topology. For every $y \in E$ with $y \ne 0$, the support function of the whole space
$F$ is $+\infty$ at $y$: $$\sup_{w \in F} B(y, w) = +\infty.$$

The separation hypothesis is needed: with the indiscrete topology on $E$ the continuous dual is
$0$, and every support function vanishes identically. -/
theorem supportFn_univ_of_ne_zero [SeparatingDual ℝ E] {y : E} (hy : y ≠ 0) :
    supportFn B.flip (univ : Set F) y = ⊤ := by
  by_contra hne
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := supportFn_lt_top_iff.1 (lt_top_iff_ne_top.2 hne)
  obtain ⟨g, hg⟩ := SeparatingDual.exists_ne_zero (R := ℝ) hy
  obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := exists_pairing_eq B g
  have hBw : B y w ≠ 0 := by rw [← hw y]; exact hg
  have hstep : B y (((c + 1) / B y w) • w) ≤ c := hc _ (mem_univ _)
  rw [map_smul, smul_eq_mul, div_mul_cancel₀ _ hBw] at hstep
  linarith

omit [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] in
/-- **The conjugate of the constant function $0$ is $\delta(\cdot \mid 0)$.** Under the hypotheses
of `ConvexAnalysis.supportFn_univ_of_ne_zero`, the conjugate of the zero function on $F$, for
the flipped pairing, is the indicator function of $\{0\} \subseteq E$.

This is the converse of `ConvexAnalysis.convexConj_indicatorFn_zero`. It carries the separation
hypothesis of `ConvexAnalysis.supportFn_univ_of_ne_zero`, because $0$ is the indicator function
of the whole space. -/
theorem convexConj_zero_eq_indicatorFn [SeparatingDual ℝ E] :
    convexConj B.flip (0 : F → EReal) = indicatorFn ({0} : Set E) := by
  have hzero : (0 : F → EReal) = indicatorFn (univ : Set F) := by
    funext x
    rw [indicatorFn_of_mem (mem_univ x)]
    rfl
  rw [hzero, ← supportFn_eq_convexConj_indicatorFn]
  funext y
  by_cases hy : y = 0
  · subst hy
    rw [indicatorFn_of_mem (Set.mem_singleton _), supportFn_zero ⟨0, mem_univ 0⟩]
  · rw [indicatorFn_of_notMem (by simpa using hy), supportFn_univ_of_ne_zero (B := B) hy]

/-- **Co-finiteness, in the form that holds in infinite dimensions.** Let $E$ and $F$ be locally
convex spaces, let $E$ have separating dual, and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with the
topologies of both $E$ and $F$. For a closed proper convex $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$,
$$\operatorname{cl} (\operatorname{dom} f^*) = F \iff (f 0^+)(y) = +\infty \text{ for every } y
\ne 0.$$

The statement $\operatorname{dom} f^* = \mathbb{R}^n$ of the book is strictly stronger outside
finite dimensions: the kernel of a discontinuous linear functional is a dense proper convex
subset on which no nonzero continuous linear functional is bounded above.
`ConvexAnalysis.convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff` gives the form of the book when $F$ is
finite-dimensional. -/
theorem closure_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff [SeparatingDual ℝ E]
    (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    closure (convexDom (convexConj B f)) = univ ↔ ∀ y : E, y ≠ 0 → recessionFn f y = ⊤ := by
  have hrec := recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf
  constructor
  · intro h y hy
    have := congrFun hrec y
    rw [this, ← supportFn_closure (B := B.flip) (convexDom (convexConj B f)), h]
    exact supportFn_univ_of_ne_zero (B := B) hy
  · intro h
    by_contra hcl
    obtain ⟨φ, c, hφ, hbnd⟩ := exists_ne_zero_forall_le_of_closure_ne_univ
      (convexFn_convexConj B f).convex_convexDom (properConvex_convexConj hf).convexDom_nonempty hcl
    obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := exists_pairing_eq B.flip φ
    have hy0 : y ≠ 0 := by
      rintro rfl
      refine hφ (ContinuousLinearMap.ext fun w => ?_)
      simp [hy w]
    have hle : recessionFn f y ≤ (c : EReal) := by
      rw [congrFun hrec y, supportFn_le_coe_iff]
      exact fun w hw => (hy w).symm ▸ hbnd w hw
    rw [h y hy0] at hle
    exact absurd hle (by simp)

omit [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] in
/-- **If $E$ has separating dual, a compatible pairing separates the points of $E$.** Let the
pairing $B$ be compatible with the topology of $E$, and let $E$ have separating dual. If
$B(x, y) = 0$ for every $y \in F$, then $x = 0$.

The proof: every continuous linear functional has the form $B(\cdot, y)$, so every continuous
linear functional vanishes at $x$. Statements that quantify over the nonzero vectors of $E$ need
this. -/
theorem separatingRight_flip_of_separatingDual (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) [IsCompatiblePairing B]
    [SeparatingDual ℝ E] : B.flip.SeparatingRight := by
  intro x hx
  by_contra hne
  obtain ⟨g, hg⟩ := SeparatingDual.exists_ne_zero (R := ℝ) hne
  obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := exists_pairing_eq B g
  exact hg (by rw [hy x]; simpa using hx y)

omit [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] in
/-- **If $E$ has separating dual, a compatible pairing is injective on the left.** Let the pairing
$B$ be compatible with the topology of $E$, and let $E$ have separating dual. Then the map
$x \mapsto B(x, \cdot)$ is injective.

This is `ConvexAnalysis.separatingRight_flip_of_separatingDual` as injectivity of the bilinear
map. This is the form that the uniqueness results, which are stated with no topology at all,
take as an explicit hypothesis. Over a normed space `SeparatingDual ℝ E` holds automatically, so
compatibility alone suffices there. -/
theorem injective_of_separatingDual (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) [IsCompatiblePairing B]
    [SeparatingDual ℝ E] : Function.Injective B := by
  intro x₁ x₂ h
  refine sub_eq_zero.1 (separatingRight_flip_of_separatingDual B (x₁ - x₂) fun z => ?_)
  rw [LinearMap.flip_apply, map_sub, LinearMap.sub_apply, h, sub_self]

/-!
### Where a point sits relative to $\operatorname{dom} f^*$

Whether a point lies in the closure, the relative interior, the interior or the affine hull of
$\operatorname{dom} f^*$ is decided by the recession function of $f$. The proofs use the
description, by support functions, of each of these four positions relative to a convex set,
together with `ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj`.

The book states the four clauses for the translated function $g = f - \langle \cdot, y_0 \rangle$,
whose recession function is $g 0^+ = f 0^+ - \langle \cdot, y_0 \rangle$. The conditions
$(g 0^+)(y) \ge 0$ and $\langle y, y_0 \rangle \le (f 0^+)(y)$ are the same inequality, and the
statements below avoid naming the translation.
-/

/-- **$y_0$ lies in the closure of $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ exactly when the recession function of
$f$ lies above the linear function $\langle \cdot, y_0 \rangle$.** Let $E$ and $F$ be locally
convex spaces, and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with the topologies of both $E$ and $F$.
For a closed proper convex $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ and $y_0 \in F$, $$y_0 \in
\operatorname{cl} (\operatorname{dom} f^*) \iff B(y, y_0) \le (f 0^+)(y) \text{ for every } y
\in E.$$
-/
theorem mem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) (y₀ : F) :
    y₀ ∈ closure (convexDom (convexConj B f)) ↔ ∀ y : E,
        ((B y y₀ : ℝ) : EReal) ≤ recessionFn f y := by
  have hconv : Convex ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := (convexFn_convexConj B f).convex_convexDom
  rw [← hconv.convexHull_eq, mem_closure_convexHull_iff_le_supportFn (B := B.flip)]
  simp only [LinearMap.flip_apply, ← recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf]

/-- **The same at the origin.** Under the hypotheses of
`ConvexAnalysis.mem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff`, the origin lies in the closure of
$\operatorname{dom} f^*$ if and only if $(f 0^+)(y) \ge 0$ for every $y \in E$, that is, $f$
recedes at a negative rate in no direction. -/
theorem zero_mem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    (0 : F) ∈ closure (convexDom (convexConj B f)) ↔ ∀ y : E, 0 ≤ recessionFn f y := by
  rw [mem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff (B := B) hf]
  simp

omit [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E]
  [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E] in
/-- The recession bound $(f 0^+)(y) \le -\varepsilon$ written out. For every
$f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$, $y \in E$ and $\varepsilon \in \mathbb{R}$, $$(f 0^+)(y) \le
-\varepsilon \iff f(x + a y) \le f(x) - a \varepsilon \text{ for every } x \in
\operatorname{dom} f \text{ and } a \ge 0.$$ So $f$ decreases at rate at least $\varepsilon$
along $y$.

The restriction of $x$ to $\operatorname{dom} f$ costs nothing: off $\operatorname{dom} f$ the
right-hand side is $+\infty - a \varepsilon = +\infty$. -/
theorem recessionFn_le_neg_coe_iff (f : E → EReal) (y : E) (ε : ℝ) :
    recessionFn f y ≤ ((-ε : ℝ) : EReal) ↔
      ∀ x ∈ convexDom f, ∀ a : ℝ, 0 ≤ a → f (x + a • y) ≤ f x - ((a * ε : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  have hrw : ∀ (x : E) (a : ℝ), (f x + ((a * -ε : ℝ) : EReal))
      = f x - ((a * ε : ℝ) : EReal) := fun x a => by
    rw [show (a * -ε : ℝ) = -(a * ε) from by ring, EReal.coe_neg, ← sub_eq_add_neg]
  rw [recessionFn_le_coe_iff_forall]
  refine ⟨fun h x _ a ha => (hrw x a) ▸ h x a ha, fun h x a ha => (hrw x a).symm ▸ ?_⟩
  rcases eq_or_ne (f x) ⊤ with hx | hx
  · rw [hx, EReal.top_sub_coe]
    exact le_top
  · exact h x (mem_convexDom.2 (lt_top_iff_ne_top.2 hx)) a ha

/-- **The origin lies outside the closure of $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ exactly when $f$ decreases at
a uniform positive rate along some direction.** Under the hypotheses of
`ConvexAnalysis.mem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff`, $0 \notin \operatorname{cl}
(\operatorname{dom} f^*)$ if and only if there are $y \in E$ with $y \ne 0$ and
$\varepsilon > 0$ such that $$f(x + a y) \le f(x) - a \varepsilon \quad \text{for every } x \in
\operatorname{dom} f \text{ and } a \ge 0.$$

The condition $y \ne 0$ is automatic: at $y = 0$ the inequality at $a = 1$ would read
$0 \le -\varepsilon$ at any point of the nonempty effective domain. -/
theorem zero_notMem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    (0 : F) ∉ closure (convexDom (convexConj B f)) ↔
      ∃ y : E, y ≠ 0 ∧ ∃ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε ∧
        ∀ x ∈ convexDom f, ∀ a : ℝ, 0 ≤ a → f (x + a • y) ≤ f x - ((a * ε : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  rw [zero_mem_closure_convexDom_convexConj_iff (B := B) hf, not_forall]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨y, hy⟩
    rw [not_le] at hy
    obtain ⟨m, hm₁, hm₂⟩ := EReal.lt_iff_exists_real_btwn.1 hy
    have hmneg : m < 0 := by exact_mod_cast hm₂
    refine ⟨y, ?_, -m, neg_pos.2 hmneg, (recessionFn_le_neg_coe_iff f y (-m)).1 ?_⟩
    · rintro rfl
      rw [recessionFn_apply_zero hf.proper] at hy
      exact absurd hy (lt_irrefl 0)
    · rw [neg_neg]
      exact hm₁.le
  · rintro ⟨y, -, ε, hε, hineq⟩
    refine ⟨y, not_le.2 (lt_of_le_of_lt ((recessionFn_le_neg_coe_iff f y ε).2 hineq) ?_)⟩
    exact_mod_cast neg_lt_zero.2 hε

end CofiniteMain

section CofiniteFiniteDim

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [TopologicalSpace E]
  [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E] [SeparatingDual ℝ E]
  [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace ℝ F] [FiniteDimensional ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] {f : E → EReal}
  {D : Set F}

omit [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [SeparatingDual ℝ E] [IsCompatiblePairing B] [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] in
/-- **In finite dimensions, a dense convex set is the whole space.** Let $F$ be a finite-dimensional
real normed space and $D \subseteq F$ convex with $\operatorname{cl} D = F$. Then $D = F$.

The proof combines $\operatorname{ri} (\operatorname{cl} D) = \operatorname{ri} D$ with
$\operatorname{ri} F = F$. -/
theorem _root_.Convex.eq_univ_of_closure_eq_univ (hD : Convex ℝ D) (h : closure D = univ) :
    D = univ := by
  have h1 : ri (closure D) = ri D := Convex.relint_closure hD
  rw [h, intrinsicInterior_univ] at h1
  exact univ_subset_iff.1 (h1 ▸ intrinsicInterior_subset)

/-- **Co-finiteness in finite dimensions.** Let $E$ be a locally convex space with separating dual,
let $F$ be a finite-dimensional real normed space, and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with
the topologies of both $E$ and $F$. For a closed proper convex $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$,
the conjugate $f^*$ is finite everywhere if and only if $(f 0^+)(y) = +\infty$ for every
$y \ne 0$: $$\operatorname{dom} f^* = F \iff (f 0^+)(y) = +\infty \text{ for every } y \ne 0.$$
This is Corollary 13.3.1 of [rockafellar1970convex]. -/
theorem convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    convexDom (convexConj B f) = univ ↔ ∀ y : E, y ≠ 0 → recessionFn f y = ⊤ := by
  rw [← closure_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff (B := B) hf]
  exact ⟨fun h => by rw [h, closure_univ],
    Convex.eq_univ_of_closure_eq_univ (convexFn_convexConj B f).convex_convexDom⟩

/-- **Co-finiteness in finite dimensions**, through `ConvexAnalysis.Cofinite`. Under the hypotheses
of `ConvexAnalysis.convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff`, a closed proper convex $f$ is co-finite if
and only if $\operatorname{dom} f^* = F$. This is Corollary 13.3.1 of [rockafellar1970convex]. -/
theorem cofinite_iff_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    Cofinite f ↔ convexDom (convexConj B f) = univ := by
  rw [convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff (B := B) hf]
  exact ⟨fun h => h.recessionFn_eq_top, fun h => { hf with recessionFn_eq_top := h }⟩

/-- Under the hypotheses of `ConvexAnalysis.convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff`, a co-finite function
$f$ has $\operatorname{dom} f^* = F$: its conjugate is finite everywhere.

This is the forward half of `ConvexAnalysis.cofinite_iff_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ`, for use
with dot notation. -/
theorem Cofinite.convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ
    (hf : Cofinite f) : convexDom (convexConj B f) = univ :=
  (cofinite_iff_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ (B := B) hf.toClosedProperConvexFn).1 hf

/-- Under the hypotheses of `ConvexAnalysis.convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff`, a closed proper
convex $f$ is co-finite if and only if $f^*(y) < +\infty$ for every $y \in F$. -/
theorem cofinite_iff_forall_convexConj_lt_top (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    Cofinite f ↔ ∀ y : F, convexConj B f y < ⊤ := by
  rw [cofinite_iff_convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ (B := B) hf]
  exact ⟨fun h y => by rw [← mem_convexDom, h]; trivial, fun h => eq_univ_of_forall fun y => h y⟩

omit [SeparatingDual ℝ E] in
/-- **$y_0$ lies in the relative interior of $\operatorname{dom} f^*$** exactly when the recession
function of $f$ lies above $\langle \cdot, y_0 \rangle$, strictly in every direction in which it
is not additively reversible. Let $E$ be a locally convex space, $F$ a finite-dimensional real
normed space, and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with the topologies of both $E$ and $F$. For
a closed proper convex $f$ and $y_0 \in F$, $y_0 \in \operatorname{ri} (\operatorname{dom} f^*)$
if and only if $B(y, y_0) \le (f 0^+)(y)$ for every $y \in E$, and $$B(y, y_0) < (f 0^+)(y)
\quad \text{for every } y \text{ with } -(f 0^+)(-y) \ne (f 0^+)(y).$$

The exceptional directions, those with $-(f 0^+)(-y) = (f 0^+)(y)$, are the directions along
which $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ lies in a hyperplane. -/
theorem mem_relint_convexDom_convexConj_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) (y₀ : F) :
    y₀ ∈ ri (convexDom (convexConj B f)) ↔ (∀ y : E, ((B y y₀ : ℝ) : EReal) ≤ recessionFn f y) ∧
      ∀ y : E, -recessionFn f (-y) ≠ recessionFn f y →
        ((B y y₀ : ℝ) : EReal) < recessionFn f y := by
  have hconv : Convex ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := (convexFn_convexConj B f).convex_convexDom
  rw [mem_relint_iff_lt_supportFn (B := B.flip) hconv y₀]
  simp only [LinearMap.flip_apply, ← recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf]

/-- **$y_0$ lies in the interior of $\operatorname{dom} f^*$** exactly when the recession function
of $f$ lies strictly above $\langle \cdot, y_0 \rangle$ in every nonzero direction. Under the
hypotheses of `ConvexAnalysis.convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff`, for a closed proper convex $f$
and $y_0 \in F$, $$y_0 \in \operatorname{int} (\operatorname{dom} f^*) \iff B(y, y_0) < (f
0^+)(y) \text{ for every } y \ne 0.$$
-/
theorem mem_interior_convexDom_convexConj_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) (y₀ : F) :
    y₀ ∈ interior (convexDom (convexConj B f)) ↔
      ∀ y : E, y ≠ 0 → ((B y y₀ : ℝ) : EReal) < recessionFn f y := by
  have hconv : Convex ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := (convexFn_convexConj B f).convex_convexDom
  rw [mem_interior_iff_lt_supportFn
      (B := B.flip) hconv (properConvex_convexConj hf).convexDom_nonempty
    (separatingRight_flip_of_separatingDual B) y₀]
  simp only [LinearMap.flip_apply, ← recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf]

omit [SeparatingDual ℝ E] in
/-- **$y_0$ lies in the affine hull of $\operatorname{dom} f^*$** exactly when
$\langle \cdot, y_0 \rangle$ agrees with the recession function of $f$ in every direction in
which the latter is additively reversible. Let $E$ be a locally convex space, $F$ a
finite-dimensional real normed space, and let the pairing $B$ be compatible with the topologies
of both $E$ and $F$. For a closed proper convex $f$ and $y_0 \in F$, $$y_0 \in
\operatorname{aff} (\operatorname{dom} f^*) \iff B(y, y_0) = (f 0^+)(y) \text{ for every } y
\text{ with } -(f 0^+)(-y) = (f 0^+)(y).$$
-/
theorem mem_affineSpan_convexDom_convexConj_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) (y₀ : F) :
    y₀ ∈ affineSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) ↔
      ∀ y : E, -recessionFn f (-y) = recessionFn f y →
        ((B y y₀ : ℝ) : EReal) = recessionFn f y := by
  rw [mem_affineSpan_iff_eq_supportFn
      (B := B.flip) (properConvex_convexConj hf).convexDom_nonempty y₀]
  simp only [LinearMap.flip_apply, ← recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf]

/-- **$\operatorname{dom} f^*$ has nonempty interior exactly when the lineality space of $f$ is
trivial**, that is, when there is no line along which $f$ is finite and affine. Under the
hypotheses of `ConvexAnalysis.convexDom_convexConj_eq_univ_iff`, for a closed proper convex $f$,
$$\operatorname{int} (\operatorname{dom} f^*) \ne \emptyset \iff \operatorname{lin} f = \{0\}.$$

The lineality space $\operatorname{lin} f$ is `ConvexAnalysis.linealitySpaceFn f`. -/
theorem interior_convexDom_convexConj_nonempty_iff (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    (interior (convexDom (convexConj B f))).Nonempty ↔ linealitySpaceFn f = {0} := by
  have hconv : Convex ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := (convexFn_convexConj B f).convex_convexDom
  have hnedom : (convexDom (convexConj B f)).Nonempty :=
      (properConvex_convexConj hf).convexDom_nonempty
  rw [hconv.interior_nonempty_iff_affineSpan_eq_top,
    AffineSubspace.affineSpan_eq_top_iff_vectorSpan_eq_top_of_nonempty ℝ F F hnedom,
    linealitySpaceFn_eq_annihilator_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf]
  constructor
  · intro htop
    ext x
    simp only [Set.mem_ofPred_eq, Set.mem_singleton_iff]
    refine ⟨fun hx => separatingRight_flip_of_separatingDual B x fun w => ?_, ?_⟩
    · simpa using hx w (by rw [htop]; exact Submodule.mem_top)
    · rintro rfl w -
      simp
  · intro hlin
    by_contra hcon
    obtain ⟨w₀, hw₀⟩ : ∃ w₀ : F, w₀ ∉ vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := by
      by_contra hall
      push Not at hall
      exact hcon (eq_top_iff.2 fun w _ => hall w)
    obtain ⟨φ, u, v, hφw, huv, hφV⟩ := geometric_hahn_banach_compact_closed
      (convex_singleton w₀) isCompact_singleton (vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f))).convex
      (vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f))).closed_of_finiteDimensional
      (Set.disjoint_singleton_left.2 hw₀)
    have hzero : ∀ w ∈ vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)), φ w = 0 := by
      intro w hw
      by_contra hne
      have hb : ∀ t : ℝ, v < t * φ w := fun t => by
        simpa using hφV (t • w) ((vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f))).smul_mem t hw)
      have hcontra := hb ((v - 1) / φ w)
      rw [div_mul_cancel₀ _ hne] at hcontra
      linarith
    have hv0 : v < 0 := by
      have hz := hφV 0 (Submodule.zero_mem _)
      rwa [hzero 0 (Submodule.zero_mem _)] at hz
    have hw0ne : φ w₀ ≠ 0 := by
      have h₁ : φ w₀ < u := hφw w₀ rfl
      linarith
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := exists_pairing_eq B.flip φ
    have hbx : ∀ w : F, B x w = φ w := fun w => (hx w).symm
    have hxne : x ≠ 0 := by
      rintro rfl
      exact hw0ne (by rw [← hbx w₀]; simp)
    have hmem : x ∈ {z : E | ∀ w ∈ vectorSpan ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)), B z w = 0} :=
      fun w hw => by rw [hbx w]; exact hzero w hw
    rw [hlin] at hmem
    exact hxne hmem

end CofiniteFiniteDim

end ConvexAnalysis
